1
00:00:03,564 --> 00:00:06,746
[SPEAKER_01]: I was wondering if we could talk about anicca once again.

2
00:00:08,467 --> 00:00:09,608
[SPEAKER_00]: It's been a while.

3
00:00:11,269 --> 00:00:25,258
[SPEAKER_01]: And I found some suttas pointed out by another friend, which shows where the... how to develop or

4
00:00:26,557 --> 00:00:35,100
[SPEAKER_01]: Where that perception of anicca should be developed, how it should be developed, what's the importance of it?

5
00:00:35,160 --> 00:00:38,202
[SPEAKER_01]: Because there are these three things.

6
00:00:38,242 --> 00:00:42,683
[SPEAKER_01]: The view of satisfaction, the view of self and wrong view.

7
00:00:45,985 --> 00:00:51,147
[SPEAKER_01]: And these other three things are to be developed for abandoning those three things.

8
00:00:52,307 --> 00:00:53,768
[SPEAKER_01]: The perception of impermanence,

9
00:00:55,465 --> 00:00:59,266
[SPEAKER_01]: Anicca used to be developed for abandoning the view of satisfaction.

10
00:01:00,426 --> 00:01:02,647
[SPEAKER_01]: So, that's the perception.

11
00:01:02,687 --> 00:01:07,588
[SPEAKER_01]: Anicca used to be developed to abandon the view that things are satisfying.

12
00:01:07,948 --> 00:01:08,828
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah.

13
00:01:09,889 --> 00:01:15,310
[SPEAKER_01]: So, I have a view that this is satisfying my situation.

14
00:01:15,350 --> 00:01:15,850
[SPEAKER_01]: It's great.

15
00:01:16,370 --> 00:01:19,571
[SPEAKER_01]: I like, you know, my body is good.

16
00:01:20,011 --> 00:01:20,792
[SPEAKER_01]: It's working.

17
00:01:21,352 --> 00:01:21,952
[SPEAKER_01]: I'm satisfied.

18
00:01:23,015 --> 00:01:26,958
[SPEAKER_00]: My life, my family, my views, my convictions are satisfying to me.

19
00:01:30,160 --> 00:01:48,294
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah, that's why, like, when, as this other suttas say, like, you know, when samanas and brāhmaṇas live withdrawn from sensuality physically, but then they start falling into views, like, really start holding the views, because that becomes where they find their safety and satisfaction and comfort in their convictions.

20
00:01:50,095 --> 00:01:50,335
[SPEAKER_00]: So,

21
00:01:52,573 --> 00:01:57,396
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah, you could see, like, well, we said that before, I think, obviously, in many other talks.

22
00:01:58,076 --> 00:02:02,979
[SPEAKER_00]: Anicca needs to be... can only be understood on that personal level.

23
00:02:03,179 --> 00:02:14,885
[SPEAKER_00]: So, when you find things that are dear to you and contemplate their cessation or change, the feeling that you get as a result of it, well, that's the closest to anicca you can get in the beginning.

24
00:02:14,925 --> 00:02:17,246
[SPEAKER_00]: And that's the direction to go.

25
00:02:17,286 --> 00:02:22,009
[SPEAKER_00]: But the problem is, obviously, with like a, you know, contemporary Buddhism, the whole kind of

26
00:02:22,728 --> 00:02:44,102
[SPEAKER_00]: Mass of commentaries and, like, scholarly interpretation of terminologies and so on, that's not paired with the fundamental practice and the right view, then you get these ideas of the scientific, that things are constantly changing and that's why they're not real and that's why dukkha is not real.

27
00:02:46,804 --> 00:02:49,706
[SPEAKER_00]: But you realize, well, then the Buddha would have simply said that.

28
00:02:49,999 --> 00:02:54,502
[SPEAKER_00]: He said everything is constantly changing and none of your pain is real and nothing is ever real.

29
00:02:54,842 --> 00:02:55,382
[SPEAKER_00]: But he didn't.

30
00:02:57,403 --> 00:03:07,869
[SPEAKER_00]: He said contemplate impermanence to overcome satisfaction, to abandon satisfaction in things, in dhammas, in views, in possessions.

31
00:03:09,350 --> 00:03:11,791
[SPEAKER_00]: And you could realize anybody can adopt the view.

32
00:03:12,512 --> 00:03:16,454
[SPEAKER_00]: Any scientist who is measuring, sort of physicist or whatever,

33
00:03:16,780 --> 00:03:22,602
[SPEAKER_00]: That it's measuring data, measuring things, measuring electrons constantly moving, would say, oh, things are constantly changing.

34
00:03:23,122 --> 00:03:24,343
[SPEAKER_00]: Anybody can develop that view.

35
00:03:24,363 --> 00:03:25,483
[SPEAKER_00]: Anybody can adopt that view.

36
00:03:26,383 --> 00:03:27,584
[SPEAKER_00]: No prerequisites needed.

37
00:03:28,264 --> 00:03:30,885
[SPEAKER_00]: You just need to hear it and need to be pleased about it, and that's it.

38
00:03:32,946 --> 00:03:34,306
[SPEAKER_00]: You don't need to endure anything.

39
00:03:34,546 --> 00:03:36,167
[SPEAKER_00]: You don't need to keep the precepts.

40
00:03:36,347 --> 00:03:43,590
[SPEAKER_00]: You don't need to... well, you don't need to abandon safety that you find in the world and all the satisfaction.

41
00:03:43,610 --> 00:03:44,450
[SPEAKER_00]: You just adopt the view.

42
00:03:45,443 --> 00:03:46,563
[SPEAKER_00]: That everything is constantly changing.

43
00:03:46,623 --> 00:03:59,006
[SPEAKER_00]: And, yeah, often people like that, people that follow commentaries and and kind of contemporary interpretations, would often try and connect and say, yes, of course, modern science has proven that everything is constantly changing.

44
00:03:59,026 --> 00:04:00,427
[SPEAKER_00]: And, see, the Buddha also said that.

45
00:04:00,947 --> 00:04:04,988
[SPEAKER_00]: So, the modern science has proven that the Buddha was right, as if such thing is possible.

46
00:04:05,568 --> 00:04:07,328
[SPEAKER_00]: Putujana can prove an arahant right.

47
00:04:07,869 --> 00:04:09,769
[SPEAKER_00]: That's just insane.

48
00:04:10,409 --> 00:04:13,530
[SPEAKER_00]: So, so the whole the whole idea of, like,

49
00:04:14,566 --> 00:04:18,070
[SPEAKER_00]: Things constantly changing, and everything is constant flux, and it is linear.

50
00:04:18,191 --> 00:04:25,580
[SPEAKER_00]: A leads to B, leads to C, leads to D, and that's how everything is going and moving, and one to the next, and that's why nothing is real, that's why nothing is permanent.

51
00:04:26,743 --> 00:04:33,967
[SPEAKER_00]: That doesn't require anything, nor would that view uproot your satisfaction in the world.

52
00:04:34,428 --> 00:04:35,308
[SPEAKER_00]: It would not make a difference.

53
00:04:35,328 --> 00:04:43,073
[SPEAKER_00]: It would just provide a new gloss, the latest veneer of kind of hindsight interpretation of what it is.

54
00:04:43,793 --> 00:04:45,774
[SPEAKER_00]: But things can never be experienced like that.

55
00:04:46,795 --> 00:04:50,997
[SPEAKER_00]: Like, if things are constantly changing, second to second, you would not be a self-conscious being.

56
00:04:51,438 --> 00:04:55,480
[SPEAKER_00]: There would be no steadier reference point that's not changing as much.

57
00:04:58,623 --> 00:05:05,227
[SPEAKER_00]: You would simply be swept from A to B and C and there would be no... there would not even be a notion of memory.

58
00:05:07,508 --> 00:05:24,157
[SPEAKER_00]: So, the whole point is... and see, like, again, when people adopt that view, although they might draw it from science or from whatever, commentary, explanation and so on, it's not... it does not remain just merely intellectual.

59
00:05:24,877 --> 00:05:27,819
[SPEAKER_00]: There is the whole kind of emotional side invested in that.

60
00:05:28,523 --> 00:05:47,947
[SPEAKER_00]: Because people would have arrived at the Buddha's teaching, because they want to be free from suffering, but then they jumped into this view, that then, as I said, retroactively describes what happens to you, which is why nothing is real, which is why you don't suffer, and then they get emotionally invested in that.

61
00:05:48,987 --> 00:05:50,768
[SPEAKER_00]: Because they are suffering?

62
00:05:51,108 --> 00:05:51,728
[SPEAKER_00]: Well, yes.

63
00:05:52,188 --> 00:05:54,688
[SPEAKER_00]: Because, basically, they want to be free from suffering.

64
00:05:55,388 --> 00:05:57,969
[SPEAKER_00]: So they want to be free from suffering, so they invest

65
00:05:58,742 --> 00:06:02,604
[SPEAKER_00]: In this explanation that tells them that their suffering is not real, because everything is constantly changing.

66
00:06:03,404 --> 00:06:10,088
[SPEAKER_00]: So, it's not going to be easy for them to then just drop that view, because that means now they have to drop their whole idea of practice.

67
00:06:10,748 --> 00:06:15,411
[SPEAKER_00]: Their whole idea of that kind of relief that they got from the belief that their suffering is not real.

68
00:06:17,211 --> 00:06:18,212
[SPEAKER_00]: But that is necessary.

69
00:06:19,869 --> 00:06:29,374
[SPEAKER_00]: If they were to actually start to understand the endurance... Because, you know, when the Buddha described, there is... there is arising is manifest, ceasing is manifest, and persisting while changing is manifest.

70
00:06:29,574 --> 00:06:30,854
[SPEAKER_00]: The three characteristics.

71
00:06:32,615 --> 00:06:34,716
[SPEAKER_00]: So, persisting while changing means exactly that.

72
00:06:35,717 --> 00:06:39,138
[SPEAKER_00]: Persisting as the same thing while changing on a different level.

73
00:06:39,318 --> 00:06:40,619
[SPEAKER_00]: And you realize everything, everything.

74
00:06:40,639 --> 00:06:43,520
[SPEAKER_00]: So, it's like my intention to sit here and talk to you.

75
00:06:46,202 --> 00:06:47,242
[SPEAKER_00]: It remains persisting.

76
00:06:48,541 --> 00:06:50,582
[SPEAKER_00]: Because if I change my mind, I'll get up and walk out.

77
00:06:52,142 --> 00:07:01,806
[SPEAKER_00]: So, all these other things that are constantly changing, my arms are moving, my mouth is moving, I'm speaking, different sounds are coming out, different questions, different ideas, all of that is on a lower level.

78
00:07:02,407 --> 00:07:06,948
[SPEAKER_00]: So, that is changing, while my intention to remain seated, talking to you, is not changing.

79
00:07:08,749 --> 00:07:10,550
[SPEAKER_00]: And if you were to say, well, that intention

80
00:07:11,278 --> 00:07:14,700
[SPEAKER_00]: To remain seated here and talk to you, well, that's just an idea.

81
00:07:14,760 --> 00:07:16,661
[SPEAKER_00]: That's just... Well, it is an idea.

82
00:07:17,021 --> 00:07:17,501
[SPEAKER_00]: It's an image.

83
00:07:17,541 --> 00:07:18,261
[SPEAKER_00]: It's real as such.

84
00:07:19,142 --> 00:07:27,626
[SPEAKER_00]: And furthermore, if there is not idea in my mind to sit here and talk to you, none of these smaller things that I do would be intelligible.

85
00:07:27,766 --> 00:07:28,726
[SPEAKER_00]: They would make no sense.

86
00:07:29,667 --> 00:07:31,648
[SPEAKER_00]: Because there would be no unifying idea.

87
00:07:31,988 --> 00:07:36,730
[SPEAKER_00]: There would be no idea that persists while these things within that idea are changing.

88
00:07:38,160 --> 00:07:39,120
[SPEAKER_00]: So, that's the whole point.

89
00:07:39,541 --> 00:08:07,371
[SPEAKER_00]: Then, a person, if they were to... if they were to basically overcome their scientific view of everything is in a constant flux and changing from A to B to C and nothing is real and it's just empty concepts, they... like, from where they're standing, when they hear the third characteristic that the Buddha talked about, persisting while remaining the same... sorry, changing while remaining the same or persisting while changing, depending on translation, that would, to them, feel like eternity, like that's permanent.

90
00:08:08,042 --> 00:08:09,183
[SPEAKER_00]: Oh, but that's all, that's permanent.

91
00:08:09,283 --> 00:08:09,983
[SPEAKER_00]: It's like, well, yeah.

92
00:08:10,763 --> 00:08:14,606
[SPEAKER_00]: So, first you have to now adopt the idea that things are permanent.

93
00:08:17,307 --> 00:08:18,247
[SPEAKER_00]: And see how that feels.

94
00:08:20,649 --> 00:08:25,491
[SPEAKER_00]: And then, start seeing, okay, but that permanence is not mine.

95
00:08:26,532 --> 00:08:27,432
[SPEAKER_00]: It's not for me.

96
00:08:27,813 --> 00:08:28,513
[SPEAKER_00]: It's not I am.

97
00:08:29,534 --> 00:08:33,436
[SPEAKER_00]: And that's the closest, that's much closer to the impermanence.

98
00:08:33,676 --> 00:08:35,757
[SPEAKER_00]: To actual anicca, the putujana can come.

99
00:08:36,834 --> 00:08:38,415
[SPEAKER_00]: So, it's not about denying things.

100
00:08:39,055 --> 00:08:43,918
[SPEAKER_00]: It's about removing your satisfaction and sense of safety that's found in them.

101
00:08:44,479 --> 00:08:54,905
[SPEAKER_00]: So, ironically enough, the view that everything is constantly changing, moment to moment, flux, consciousness, just coming and going, coming and going, is obviously a thing that's not changing.

102
00:08:55,946 --> 00:08:59,028
[SPEAKER_00]: Their view is not changing on that level.

103
00:09:00,288 --> 00:09:03,770
[SPEAKER_00]: And that's basically where they find satisfaction.

104
00:09:04,571 --> 00:09:05,852
[SPEAKER_00]: In that explanation.

105
00:09:06,682 --> 00:09:16,944
[SPEAKER_00]: Of how things are, of what's happening to me, of why my suffering is not real, and if I stick to it, all the concepts will just disappear in my mind, and I would be left with a pure reality.

106
00:09:17,084 --> 00:09:19,725
[SPEAKER_00]: It's like, well, pure reality is just another concept you just used.

107
00:09:21,645 --> 00:09:23,005
[SPEAKER_00]: And the concepts were never the problem.

108
00:09:23,725 --> 00:09:25,226
[SPEAKER_00]: That's again what the Buddha said himself.

109
00:09:25,626 --> 00:09:32,607
[SPEAKER_00]: As far as the language goes, as far as the words and designations go, that's how far Tathāgata has explained the dhamma.

110
00:09:33,328 --> 00:09:33,888
[SPEAKER_00]: There's no issue.

111
00:09:33,908 --> 00:09:37,230
[SPEAKER_00]: There's no need to say our words are just mere concepts.

112
00:09:37,250 --> 00:09:44,213
[SPEAKER_00]: There is a real reality which you just express through words, which is a mere concept, which means real reality is just mere concepts then as well.

113
00:09:45,454 --> 00:09:50,716
[SPEAKER_00]: How can you possibly perceive anything that would not involve a notion of a concept of it?

114
00:09:51,777 --> 00:09:59,621
[SPEAKER_00]: And he said, as far as the designation goes, as far as nāmarūpa goes, as far as the words and language goes, the Tāgata has understood it and explained it.

115
00:09:59,761 --> 00:10:00,461
[SPEAKER_00]: That's it.

116
00:10:00,641 --> 00:10:01,942
[SPEAKER_00]: Not less, not more than that.

117
00:10:02,737 --> 00:10:13,045
[SPEAKER_00]: If somebody claims that there is more that they can explain, more than your language can convey, or less, they don't know what they're talking about.

118
00:10:14,866 --> 00:10:16,087
[SPEAKER_00]: That's not possible, he said.

119
00:10:16,427 --> 00:10:17,508
[SPEAKER_00]: Such thing is not possible.

120
00:10:18,849 --> 00:10:24,273
[SPEAKER_00]: So, if somebody wants to contemplate anicca, exactly, find

121
00:10:26,160 --> 00:10:38,987
[SPEAKER_00]: Things, experiences in your life, that you are finding satisfaction in, they're satisfying for you, that you find safety in, all of that, and see it real as such.

122
00:10:39,407 --> 00:10:40,568
[SPEAKER_00]: There is no reason to deny it.

123
00:10:41,268 --> 00:10:45,011
[SPEAKER_00]: There is no reason to to to undermine it with these explanatory views.

124
00:10:45,051 --> 00:10:49,673
[SPEAKER_00]: Oh, but that's not real, because electrons are constantly moving and and collapse or whatever else.

125
00:10:49,733 --> 00:10:50,093
[SPEAKER_00]: No, no, no.

126
00:10:50,774 --> 00:10:51,334
[SPEAKER_00]: It's real as such.

127
00:10:51,374 --> 00:10:52,955
[SPEAKER_00]: Otherwise, you wouldn't be finding safety in it.

128
00:10:53,715 --> 00:10:54,816
[SPEAKER_00]: So, what's the problem then?

129
00:10:56,292 --> 00:11:03,116
[SPEAKER_00]: The problem then is, when you reflexively recognize your situation, oh, I am finding safety.

130
00:11:03,637 --> 00:11:06,999
[SPEAKER_00]: Say, like the typical example would be, I'm finding safety in my family.

131
00:11:07,939 --> 00:11:09,320
[SPEAKER_00]: My family is my satisfaction.

132
00:11:10,161 --> 00:11:12,522
[SPEAKER_00]: Things I work for, things I produce, things I have.

133
00:11:13,283 --> 00:11:14,463
[SPEAKER_00]: That is my satisfaction.

134
00:11:16,745 --> 00:11:24,690
[SPEAKER_00]: But then, if you are honest and you recognize that your satisfaction rests upon these things that are fundamentally not in your control,

135
00:11:26,141 --> 00:11:27,202
[SPEAKER_00]: And then you feel anxiety.

136
00:11:27,782 --> 00:11:28,783
[SPEAKER_00]: Then you feel the dread.

137
00:11:29,083 --> 00:11:31,985
[SPEAKER_00]: Then you feel, what if I lose these things?

138
00:11:32,065 --> 00:11:32,586
[SPEAKER_00]: Oh, suffering.

139
00:11:32,626 --> 00:11:33,746
[SPEAKER_00]: How do I free from suffering?

140
00:11:34,187 --> 00:11:37,769
[SPEAKER_00]: Well, you could go the route of, as I said, commentarial ideas.

141
00:11:38,069 --> 00:11:39,751
[SPEAKER_00]: They had nothing to do with the early texts.

142
00:11:40,331 --> 00:11:44,674
[SPEAKER_00]: They were introduced much later, of deny the reality of those things.

143
00:11:44,834 --> 00:11:47,076
[SPEAKER_00]: Say, your son, your daughter, they're not real.

144
00:11:47,096 --> 00:11:48,417
[SPEAKER_00]: They're just electrons and stuff.

145
00:11:49,337 --> 00:11:52,700
[SPEAKER_00]: And hammer in that until you kind of deny any

146
00:11:53,802 --> 00:11:55,964
[SPEAKER_00]: Dependence on it, while you're still dependent on it.

147
00:11:56,805 --> 00:12:02,571
[SPEAKER_00]: Because no amount of saying it's just electrons would make you not feel suffering if you were to lose them there and then.

148
00:12:04,252 --> 00:12:07,436
[SPEAKER_00]: In hindsight, then, you would explain that that wasn't suffering, it was just this.

149
00:12:07,496 --> 00:12:09,718
[SPEAKER_00]: But, first and foremost, you will feel it.

150
00:12:11,208 --> 00:12:15,429
[SPEAKER_00]: Or, go the route that actually the Buddha said you should go, if you want to prove the suffering.

151
00:12:15,909 --> 00:12:21,811
[SPEAKER_00]: Recognize that your satisfaction, your safety, depends upon those things that are not in your control.

152
00:12:22,271 --> 00:12:23,771
[SPEAKER_00]: Allow that feeling to endure.

153
00:12:24,791 --> 00:12:28,892
[SPEAKER_00]: How can mind endure that feeling of what if I lose everything that's dear to me?

154
00:12:29,332 --> 00:12:31,113
[SPEAKER_00]: Through purification of virtue.

155
00:12:32,393 --> 00:12:38,574
[SPEAKER_00]: So, through sense restraint and virtue, your mind becomes stronger and is able to endure such anxious thoughts and possibilities.

156
00:12:39,355 --> 00:12:40,535
[SPEAKER_00]: And then, gets to see them.

157
00:12:41,341 --> 00:12:50,165
[SPEAKER_00]: Even the dearest of those things, the dearest of assumptions, the dearest of perceptions that I had, have, cannot be owned in and of itself.

158
00:12:51,006 --> 00:12:51,826
[SPEAKER_00]: It is unownable.

159
00:12:52,486 --> 00:12:53,927
[SPEAKER_00]: Mind doesn't move in face of it.

160
00:12:54,067 --> 00:12:55,628
[SPEAKER_00]: Mind is not afraid from it anymore.

161
00:12:55,668 --> 00:12:56,868
[SPEAKER_00]: Like, what if...

162
00:12:56,948 --> 00:12:57,989
[SPEAKER_00]: I better stop thinking about it.

163
00:12:58,009 --> 00:13:00,850
[SPEAKER_00]: I better either explain that they're not real or distract myself from it.

164
00:13:00,910 --> 00:13:01,050
[SPEAKER_00]: No.

165
00:13:02,011 --> 00:13:04,952
[SPEAKER_00]: You remain with those things presently enduring for you.

166
00:13:06,210 --> 00:13:12,014
[SPEAKER_00]: So, that pressure, those feelings, the anxiety, what if I lose things that I love, that remains there.

167
00:13:12,654 --> 00:13:20,780
[SPEAKER_00]: And the mind stops fighting them, stops trying to sort of deny them, or stops trying to look away.

168
00:13:21,480 --> 00:13:27,244
[SPEAKER_00]: And then you get to see, well, it has a reason on its own, even the notion of attachment.

169
00:13:28,025 --> 00:13:29,385
[SPEAKER_00]: As such, it's not in my control.

170
00:13:29,505 --> 00:13:30,986
[SPEAKER_00]: As such, it is impermanent.

171
00:13:31,907 --> 00:13:33,088
[SPEAKER_00]: Whatever has come to be,

172
00:13:35,029 --> 00:13:37,831
[SPEAKER_00]: Because it's there, that is its impermanence.

173
00:13:38,231 --> 00:13:41,853
[SPEAKER_00]: Its manifestation is its unknown ability.

174
00:13:42,893 --> 00:13:44,334
[SPEAKER_00]: Because you didn't create things.

175
00:13:44,734 --> 00:13:46,315
[SPEAKER_00]: You just find them presented to you.

176
00:13:47,476 --> 00:13:49,457
[SPEAKER_00]: Then you get attached to everything else, sure.

177
00:13:50,077 --> 00:13:52,699
[SPEAKER_00]: But all that's secondary to their manifestation.

178
00:13:53,319 --> 00:13:54,800
[SPEAKER_00]: So that's the Sotāpanna's understanding.

179
00:13:54,820 --> 00:14:01,083
[SPEAKER_00]: Whatever is of the nature to manifest, it's because of that manifestation that it's impermanent.

180
00:14:01,924 --> 00:14:03,845
[SPEAKER_00]: Not because I will observe its ending.

181
00:14:05,579 --> 00:14:12,383
[SPEAKER_00]: And that's also another problem with that whole commentarial notion of things constantly in a flux of change.

182
00:14:13,004 --> 00:14:16,866
[SPEAKER_00]: It means you need to observe things ending to know the impermanence.

183
00:14:17,987 --> 00:14:21,449
[SPEAKER_00]: But that doesn't apply to any things of the personal matter.

184
00:14:21,729 --> 00:14:23,510
[SPEAKER_00]: How can you observe your life ending?

185
00:14:24,450 --> 00:14:30,534
[SPEAKER_00]: So, in other words, if you want to understand the commentarial type of impermanence, you need to die.

186
00:14:31,635 --> 00:14:32,475
[SPEAKER_00]: Because you're still alive.

187
00:14:33,976 --> 00:14:35,277
[SPEAKER_00]: Your life is still here, enduring.

188
00:14:37,611 --> 00:14:38,291
[SPEAKER_00]: It's not ending.

189
00:14:38,892 --> 00:14:41,973
[SPEAKER_00]: So, how can you then be free from the five aggregates and saṃsāra?

190
00:14:42,674 --> 00:14:46,496
[SPEAKER_00]: Because, by that view, the ending means you need to observe it gone.

191
00:14:47,957 --> 00:14:48,737
[SPEAKER_00]: But that's impossible.

192
00:14:49,878 --> 00:14:53,900
[SPEAKER_00]: So, quite the opposite, anicca does not require things to disappear.

193
00:14:53,960 --> 00:14:55,361
[SPEAKER_00]: It actually requires them to be there.

194
00:14:56,762 --> 00:15:00,764
[SPEAKER_00]: That's why the Sotāpanna's understanding is, whatever is of the nature to manifest,

195
00:15:01,550 --> 00:15:02,671
[SPEAKER_00]: That's why it's impermanent.

196
00:15:02,771 --> 00:15:05,953
[SPEAKER_00]: Not whatever was on the nature to be here and it's now gone.

197
00:15:06,233 --> 00:15:07,514
[SPEAKER_00]: That's why it's impermanent.

198
00:15:07,634 --> 00:15:08,014
[SPEAKER_00]: No, no, no.

199
00:15:08,375 --> 00:15:11,997
[SPEAKER_00]: Whatever's here, because it's here, that's why it's unownable.

200
00:15:12,297 --> 00:15:13,398
[SPEAKER_00]: That's why it's impermanent.

201
00:15:14,379 --> 00:15:15,700
[SPEAKER_01]: It's of the nature.

202
00:15:15,800 --> 00:15:21,303
[SPEAKER_00]: So, actually, you should practice impermanence before things change and disappear.

203
00:15:21,484 --> 00:15:21,664
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah.

204
00:15:21,884 --> 00:15:23,685
[SPEAKER_00]: Because if you don't... Well, exactly.

205
00:15:23,705 --> 00:15:24,325
[SPEAKER_00]: You won't see it.

206
00:15:25,326 --> 00:15:27,928
[SPEAKER_01]: You're attached to those things that you find satisfaction in.

207
00:15:28,807 --> 00:15:29,007
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah.

208
00:15:29,047 --> 00:15:32,869
[SPEAKER_01]: Because you don't see the nature of being subject to change.

209
00:15:32,989 --> 00:15:35,430
[SPEAKER_00]: Subject to change while not changing.

210
00:15:35,490 --> 00:15:35,650
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah.

211
00:15:35,950 --> 00:15:36,570
[SPEAKER_00]: That's the point.

212
00:15:36,931 --> 00:15:37,131
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah.

213
00:15:39,252 --> 00:15:46,115
[SPEAKER_01]: So, you have something that you're attached to, your family, whatever, your possessions, your view.

214
00:15:47,055 --> 00:15:47,775
[SPEAKER_00]: Your views?

215
00:15:48,476 --> 00:15:49,056
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah.

216
00:15:49,136 --> 00:15:57,620
[SPEAKER_00]: The satisfaction and safety you get on account of you that explains how everything happens, how everything ties in.

217
00:15:57,920 --> 00:15:58,160
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah.

218
00:15:59,212 --> 00:16:00,773
[SPEAKER_00]: That is, again, you find safety in it.

219
00:16:02,634 --> 00:16:04,194
[SPEAKER_00]: And that's why people withhold such views.

220
00:16:04,334 --> 00:16:06,335
[SPEAKER_00]: Oh, it's this leads to that, leads to that.

221
00:16:06,535 --> 00:16:11,198
[SPEAKER_00]: That's why always people want to understand things that happen to them, especially things that cause suffering.

222
00:16:11,238 --> 00:16:13,399
[SPEAKER_00]: Like, I want to know why it happened.

223
00:16:14,319 --> 00:16:16,900
[SPEAKER_00]: Why did he say such and such thing?

224
00:16:16,960 --> 00:16:18,921
[SPEAKER_00]: Why did she do such and such thing?

225
00:16:19,522 --> 00:16:28,666
[SPEAKER_00]: And having to, like, having some sort of retroactive, kind of in hindsight, extra information feels like, okay, I understood it, I'm fine now.

226
00:16:29,263 --> 00:16:32,305
[SPEAKER_00]: But, as the Buddha said, that's a completely wrong way to go about it.

227
00:16:32,365 --> 00:16:33,686
[SPEAKER_00]: You get shot by an arrow.

228
00:16:34,306 --> 00:16:46,995
[SPEAKER_00]: Instead of trying to figure out why he shot you, what was his plan, what was his reason, what did I do to him, you put all those questions aside and say, get this arrow out, because I'll die.

229
00:16:49,142 --> 00:16:52,684
[SPEAKER_00]: But, the foolish man does not let the doctor get the arrow out.

230
00:16:53,265 --> 00:16:59,309
[SPEAKER_00]: In other words, he doesn't do the gradual training, here and now, to get the arrow out, until he has all these answers first met.

231
00:16:59,449 --> 00:17:03,872
[SPEAKER_00]: Why, and how, and what led to this, and how A led to B, and all of that.

232
00:17:04,573 --> 00:17:09,816
[SPEAKER_00]: And you realize, oh yeah, because those views, those information that you collect, provides you a sense of safety.

233
00:17:09,856 --> 00:17:15,060
[SPEAKER_00]: You derive satisfaction from it, to the point that it does actually alleviate suffering.

234
00:17:16,021 --> 00:17:18,183
[SPEAKER_00]: You know, somebody said something so hurtful to you.

235
00:17:18,243 --> 00:17:18,964
[SPEAKER_00]: Manage.

236
00:17:19,324 --> 00:17:32,036
[SPEAKER_00]: Somebody, something said so hurtful to you, and it's like... you just... you just... you don't know how to deal with that pain, but then you find out that that person was in pain, they lost some... and suddenly you're not in pain.

237
00:17:33,097 --> 00:17:35,078
[SPEAKER_00]: It's like, well, you haven't freed yourself from suffering.

238
00:17:35,459 --> 00:17:36,199
[SPEAKER_00]: You just managed it.

239
00:17:36,259 --> 00:17:36,660
[SPEAKER_00]: Exactly.

240
00:17:37,621 --> 00:17:38,882
[SPEAKER_00]: You just changed its form.

241
00:17:39,568 --> 00:17:42,929
[SPEAKER_00]: But, you remain equally liable to somebody else saying something hurtful.

242
00:17:43,269 --> 00:17:43,849
[SPEAKER_00]: So, that means what?

243
00:17:43,949 --> 00:17:49,671
[SPEAKER_00]: Every time you need to find out what's your claim, why you shot me, why you shot somebody else, what did I do to you?

244
00:17:50,011 --> 00:17:51,752
[SPEAKER_00]: Every single time, and you will run out of time.

245
00:17:52,132 --> 00:17:53,653
[SPEAKER_00]: Sooner or later, you will run out of time.

246
00:17:56,914 --> 00:18:00,135
[SPEAKER_01]: Your view that things are permanent makes you feel good.

247
00:18:01,609 --> 00:18:02,330
[SPEAKER_00]: Well, yeah.

248
00:18:02,470 --> 00:18:03,370
[SPEAKER_00]: You don't want to think about it.

249
00:18:03,390 --> 00:18:08,213
[SPEAKER_00]: It's not an explicit, like, oh, I feel so... but it makes you feel that safety deep down.

250
00:18:08,713 --> 00:18:11,314
[SPEAKER_00]: And that's exactly what the sutta says, what it says.

251
00:18:11,374 --> 00:18:12,635
[SPEAKER_00]: Contemplate change.

252
00:18:14,097 --> 00:18:18,059
[SPEAKER_00]: So, you stop assuming permanence in things that are dear to you.

253
00:18:18,259 --> 00:18:20,219
[SPEAKER_00]: You stop assuming that things won't change.

254
00:18:20,440 --> 00:18:20,800
[SPEAKER_00]: That's it.

255
00:18:21,040 --> 00:18:25,021
[SPEAKER_01]: Because no matter how satisfying a thing is...

256
00:18:25,041 --> 00:18:28,743
[SPEAKER_01]: So, say, just keep going on family, say, attachment to family or friend.

257
00:18:29,423 --> 00:18:33,125
[SPEAKER_01]: No matter how satisfying it is, still it's of the nature.

258
00:18:33,465 --> 00:18:35,846
[SPEAKER_01]: It's on the basis of it is subject to change.

259
00:18:35,866 --> 00:18:36,526
[SPEAKER_01]: That's its nature.

260
00:18:37,326 --> 00:18:38,427
[SPEAKER_01]: So, can I...

261
00:18:39,757 --> 00:18:40,638
[SPEAKER_01]: Is that satisfying?

262
00:18:40,678 --> 00:18:42,821
[SPEAKER_01]: That it's going to be taken away at any moment?

263
00:18:43,301 --> 00:18:44,102
[SPEAKER_00]: Well, and that's the thing.

264
00:18:44,362 --> 00:18:46,184
[SPEAKER_00]: You don't need to have your family taken away.

265
00:18:47,025 --> 00:18:58,538
[SPEAKER_00]: If you contemplate, subject to change, in face of things that you don't want to change, you're emotionally invested for them not changing, you contemplate change, you realize your family would still remain there.

266
00:18:59,651 --> 00:19:02,033
[SPEAKER_00]: You don't need to get rid of them.

267
00:19:02,153 --> 00:19:04,795
[SPEAKER_00]: You don't need to have them arrested or taken away.

268
00:19:05,135 --> 00:19:11,719
[SPEAKER_00]: What will disappear is that layer of satisfaction and kind of comfort that you are finding in it.

269
00:19:11,819 --> 00:19:13,140
[SPEAKER_00]: That's the only thing that would disappear.

270
00:19:13,180 --> 00:19:19,725
[SPEAKER_00]: But obviously, for somebody who is not doing that practice, there is the framework of everything else.

271
00:19:20,145 --> 00:19:27,710
[SPEAKER_00]: Everything they experience in regard to their family and so on, is first through satisfaction, first through comfort, first through ownership.

272
00:19:28,479 --> 00:19:30,660
[SPEAKER_00]: So, that's why it's scary to contemplate these things.

273
00:19:30,740 --> 00:19:42,944
[SPEAKER_00]: Not because thinking that your family is subject to change will make them disappear this instant, but it will make your satisfaction and safety and everything else built on it, it will make that disappear in an instant.

274
00:19:43,064 --> 00:19:44,324
[SPEAKER_00]: And then what?

275
00:19:44,344 --> 00:19:44,504
[SPEAKER_00]: Exactly.

276
00:19:44,524 --> 00:19:45,324
[SPEAKER_00]: Then what am I left with?

277
00:19:45,344 --> 00:19:45,584
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah.

278
00:19:46,305 --> 00:19:47,285
[SPEAKER_01]: Non-satisfaction.

279
00:19:47,605 --> 00:19:53,291
[SPEAKER_00]: Well, then you're left with the actual anicca, yeah, and then you have a choice, as the people in the suttas have the choice.

280
00:19:53,611 --> 00:20:06,843
[SPEAKER_00]: Okay, either I will recognize this and allow it to permeate my being, whereby I will not remain attached to anything that requires my gratuitous assumption of non-changing, because such thing is not possible.

281
00:20:06,884 --> 00:20:10,107
[SPEAKER_00]: Things are subject to change, means eventually they will be taken away.

282
00:20:10,627 --> 00:20:11,388
[SPEAKER_00]: That's the whole point.

283
00:20:13,218 --> 00:20:27,782
[SPEAKER_01]: So, the only reason, the only way someone could suffer in regard to having lost something that they were attached to... Is if they were finding satisfaction and safety.

284
00:20:27,882 --> 00:20:28,882
[SPEAKER_01]: They weren't seeing anicca.

285
00:20:29,563 --> 00:20:31,143
[SPEAKER_01]: So, then, you can ask yourself, like,

286
00:20:31,998 --> 00:20:39,221
[SPEAKER_00]: If, presumably, you know, because you read the suttas, you're familiar with... you have some ideas of anicca in practice.

287
00:20:39,741 --> 00:20:47,864
[SPEAKER_00]: So, ask yourself, like, do your ideas of anicca... do they make you feel anxious?

288
00:20:47,884 --> 00:20:51,586
[SPEAKER_00]: Do they make you feel like there's nothing that you can find satisfaction and safety in?

289
00:20:52,987 --> 00:20:54,167
[SPEAKER_00]: Or do you not even feel any of that?

290
00:20:54,647 --> 00:21:01,310
[SPEAKER_00]: Everything just fits... fits into place, just everything is neatly explained, in hindsight, how everything happens, and it's all fine.

291
00:21:03,559 --> 00:21:05,060
[SPEAKER_00]: And then see.

292
00:21:05,100 --> 00:21:08,182
[SPEAKER_00]: See which one of those is you, basically.

293
00:21:09,123 --> 00:21:12,165
[SPEAKER_00]: And if you read the sutta that you just read, you realize which one you should be.

294
00:21:13,806 --> 00:21:25,734
[SPEAKER_00]: Because if those views of Anicca were right, you would be free from any... any of satisfaction, any safety in the world, any ownership, if Anicca is fully developed.

295
00:21:27,635 --> 00:21:31,758
[SPEAKER_00]: And also, you can ask yourself, like,

296
00:21:34,379 --> 00:21:44,122
[SPEAKER_00]: Would you be free from assumptions, ownership, upādānas, finding satisfaction, finding safety in things?

297
00:21:44,142 --> 00:21:52,184
[SPEAKER_00]: Would you be free from all of that without needing to constantly rehash and rehearse the explanations that tell you how everything happens?

298
00:21:53,185 --> 00:21:56,526
[SPEAKER_00]: So, when things arise, are you free?

299
00:21:56,926 --> 00:22:01,707
[SPEAKER_00]: Or do you have to go through the motions that then free you from things that have arisen?

300
00:22:01,727 --> 00:22:03,868
[SPEAKER_01]: It means you're not free, you're just managing.

301
00:22:04,607 --> 00:22:04,987
[SPEAKER_01]: That's it.

302
00:22:07,828 --> 00:22:09,148
[SPEAKER_01]: So there, I find...

303
00:22:10,088 --> 00:22:17,750
[SPEAKER_01]: I find something in my life which I find satisfaction in, that is dear to me, I'm attached to.

304
00:22:18,990 --> 00:22:23,631
[SPEAKER_01]: And then, you contemplate that that thing is subject to change.

305
00:22:23,651 --> 00:22:24,351
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah.

306
00:22:25,031 --> 00:22:26,511
[SPEAKER_01]: And you feel anxiety.

307
00:22:27,052 --> 00:22:27,212
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah.

308
00:22:28,292 --> 00:22:29,672
[SPEAKER_00]: Which means you're attached.

309
00:22:30,432 --> 00:22:30,792
[SPEAKER_00]: Exactly.

310
00:22:30,932 --> 00:22:33,513
[SPEAKER_00]: So anxiety is not a problem, as I said so many times.

311
00:22:33,553 --> 00:22:34,073
[SPEAKER_00]: It's a symptom.

312
00:22:35,002 --> 00:22:50,658
[SPEAKER_00]: So, if you contemplate change in things that you deem dear to you, that you don't want to change, and you feel anxiety, that anxiety directly corresponds to the degree of your attachment towards those things, to the degree of your gratuitous assumption of non-change.

313
00:22:51,179 --> 00:22:55,143
[SPEAKER_00]: So, you realize, okay, so, anxiety shows me what I'm doing wrong.

314
00:22:56,822 --> 00:23:03,086
[SPEAKER_00]: I stop having those notions through diligent contemplation of impermanence, anxiety will disappear.

315
00:23:03,346 --> 00:23:08,850
[SPEAKER_00]: And the diligent contemplation of impermanence is not you going through the random motions and so on.

316
00:23:09,270 --> 00:23:17,115
[SPEAKER_00]: It's through allowing your thought that this will change, allowing it to endure, not seeking to explain and get rid of it.

317
00:23:18,316 --> 00:23:20,097
[SPEAKER_00]: So, you find things that are dear to you,

318
00:23:20,932 --> 00:23:22,473
[SPEAKER_00]: Then, you contemplate that they will change.

319
00:23:22,973 --> 00:23:23,654
[SPEAKER_00]: You think about it.

320
00:23:23,694 --> 00:23:24,494
[SPEAKER_00]: You reflect on that.

321
00:23:24,974 --> 00:23:25,895
[SPEAKER_00]: Then, you have that anxiety.

322
00:23:26,035 --> 00:23:27,216
[SPEAKER_00]: And then, you leave that enduring.

323
00:23:28,416 --> 00:23:35,260
[SPEAKER_00]: Anxiety, in regard to your thoughts of things that are dear to me, that I assume they will not change, are subject to change.

324
00:23:35,740 --> 00:23:36,261
[SPEAKER_00]: And just leave it.

325
00:23:36,321 --> 00:23:38,782
[SPEAKER_00]: And even if the anxiety disappears, you leave that.

326
00:23:39,002 --> 00:23:41,944
[SPEAKER_00]: Only when you get completely distracted, forget, you bring it back up.

327
00:23:43,396 --> 00:23:53,244
[SPEAKER_00]: I needed to qualify that, because people would think then, oh, so then I just have to go through these mental exercises and methods of just repeating mantra to myself that everything... You gotta see anicca, nimitta...

328
00:23:53,425 --> 00:23:54,686
[SPEAKER_00]: Exactly, exactly, exactly.

329
00:23:54,866 --> 00:23:59,970
[SPEAKER_00]: You need to... you need to get that sense of, oh, this is subject to change.

330
00:24:00,050 --> 00:24:01,151
[SPEAKER_00]: This is unownable.

331
00:24:02,172 --> 00:24:10,279
[SPEAKER_00]: Everything that I'm... everything that exists from me, in regard to it, ownership, satisfaction, all of that, all of that,

332
00:24:10,993 --> 00:24:19,201
[SPEAKER_00]: It's contingent upon my assumption of non-change of this thing, but I have no control over it, which means my idea of non-change is ridiculous.

333
00:24:20,422 --> 00:24:25,107
[SPEAKER_00]: This is fully encapsulated in its own nature that is subject to change.

334
00:24:26,545 --> 00:24:27,525
[SPEAKER_00]: And let that endure.

335
00:24:27,725 --> 00:24:30,686
[SPEAKER_00]: Let that thought sit at the back of your mind throughout your day.

336
00:24:31,407 --> 00:24:33,067
[SPEAKER_00]: If things are subject to change, nothing else.

337
00:24:33,607 --> 00:24:41,050
[SPEAKER_00]: And then protect it through virtue and guarding of the sense doors, so you're not distracted and fall back onto finding satisfaction in things gratuitously.

338
00:24:42,050 --> 00:24:45,312
[SPEAKER_00]: You're being enamored with things, delighting in things.

339
00:24:45,512 --> 00:24:46,612
[SPEAKER_01]: That gets removed.

340
00:24:47,411 --> 00:24:48,131
[SPEAKER_00]: That withers away.

341
00:24:48,151 --> 00:24:49,012
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah, that will wither away.

342
00:24:49,032 --> 00:24:53,994
[SPEAKER_01]: Because you can't find the light in something which is, it's called dangerous to you.

343
00:24:54,815 --> 00:24:56,335
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah, well, that's the thing.

344
00:24:56,576 --> 00:25:11,863
[SPEAKER_00]: If you're doing it rightly, through the right view and correct contemplation, withering away of your sort of intoxication with life and things, that will be freeing.

345
00:25:12,304 --> 00:25:16,527
[SPEAKER_00]: The experience of that will not be, oh, I'm depressed now, because there's nothing worth of my satisfaction.

346
00:25:16,968 --> 00:25:28,216
[SPEAKER_00]: It will be, no, I'm free now, because there is nothing worth of me finding safety in it, and thus binding myself to it, and constricting my mind.

347
00:25:28,316 --> 00:25:32,860
[SPEAKER_00]: But if you're honest, you realize, okay, even if nothing's happening now, could I suffer?

348
00:25:35,321 --> 00:25:41,046
[SPEAKER_00]: And what if I don't have the ability or time to explain to myself why and how and

349
00:25:42,843 --> 00:25:43,764
[SPEAKER_01]: All these things happen.

350
00:25:44,124 --> 00:25:44,785
[SPEAKER_00]: Would I suffer?

351
00:25:44,825 --> 00:25:46,186
[SPEAKER_00]: Would I remain suffering?

352
00:25:46,206 --> 00:25:52,491
[SPEAKER_00]: What if I refrain from engaging in retroactive explanations of how my suffering came to be and which is why I'm free now?

353
00:25:52,931 --> 00:25:59,496
[SPEAKER_00]: What if I, just as an experiment, stop doing that part and just let the suffering sit here, when it arises?

354
00:26:02,018 --> 00:26:04,760
[SPEAKER_00]: Would I, would I, would I be touched by it?

355
00:26:05,220 --> 00:26:06,341
[SPEAKER_00]: Will my mind be moved by it?

356
00:26:07,562 --> 00:26:11,225
[SPEAKER_00]: Or be completely, completely above it, without any effort needed?

357
00:26:13,138 --> 00:26:22,503
[SPEAKER_00]: But, there is no amount of this explanation that you can kind of install in someone, if they lack that honesty on that personal level of knowing what's happening to them.

358
00:26:24,284 --> 00:26:25,805
[SPEAKER_00]: And, yeah, simple, again, simple question.

359
00:26:25,825 --> 00:26:35,750
[SPEAKER_00]: You can, as I said, you can quote the commentaries and you can say this and everything is... kalāpas are constantly moving and all this, but that's not how you see things.

360
00:26:35,770 --> 00:26:37,792
[SPEAKER_00]: Like, is your consciousness constantly moving?

361
00:26:37,992 --> 00:26:41,974
[SPEAKER_00]: Is your life constantly flickering on and off, on and off, on and... Like, that makes no sense.

362
00:26:43,314 --> 00:26:54,100
[SPEAKER_00]: And if you never heard that external view that you're now kind of trying to fit the suttas with and so on, it would have never crossed your mind to describe your experience like that, because that's not what your experience is.

363
00:26:56,121 --> 00:27:12,570
[SPEAKER_00]: Putting aside the whole thing that if you think you can measure motions of durations of your consciousness in moments and flickering, like what commentaries and the whole kind of contemporary and Burmese vipassana think, that would require you to basically be an observer outside of this experience as a whole.

364
00:27:12,980 --> 00:27:14,160
[SPEAKER_00]: To measure that change.

365
00:27:15,961 --> 00:27:16,721
[SPEAKER_00]: But you can't do that.

366
00:27:16,841 --> 00:27:23,422
[SPEAKER_00]: You can't even imagine the outside of the five aggregates, as the Buddha said so many times.

367
00:27:23,762 --> 00:27:29,084
[SPEAKER_00]: You're fully enclosed, so there is no outside point of view for you, in regard to yourself, nor others.

368
00:27:30,424 --> 00:27:35,665
[SPEAKER_00]: It's the aggregate of form, feeling, perception, intention and consciousness, fully encapsulated.

369
00:27:36,639 --> 00:27:38,320
[SPEAKER_00]: So, these notions of measuring...

370
00:27:38,340 --> 00:27:42,582
[SPEAKER_00]: I remember, I read that, there was this... the storage of consciousness, the collect...

371
00:27:42,602 --> 00:27:43,863
[SPEAKER_00]: It's like, how can you even know that?

372
00:27:44,883 --> 00:27:47,084
[SPEAKER_00]: When all you have is your own consciousness of phenomena?

373
00:27:48,565 --> 00:27:52,667
[SPEAKER_00]: But you come across these ideas, these phenomena, and then you ascribe this external value.

374
00:27:53,267 --> 00:27:57,009
[SPEAKER_00]: Or, this precedes me, this is the greater consciousness over there.

375
00:27:57,809 --> 00:28:05,313
[SPEAKER_00]: Or, or the view, I think, also it was in one of the early commentaries, where, where the moment-to-moment consciousness,

376
00:28:05,968 --> 00:28:08,390
[SPEAKER_00]: Which, again, is just an external idea.

377
00:28:08,990 --> 00:28:12,974
[SPEAKER_00]: These moments last like 0.0 something milliseconds.

378
00:28:13,114 --> 00:28:15,716
[SPEAKER_00]: And it's like... How can you measure that?

379
00:28:16,356 --> 00:28:18,238
[SPEAKER_00]: How can you measure your own consciousness?

380
00:28:18,258 --> 00:28:21,280
[SPEAKER_00]: How can you measure someone else's flickering moments of consciousness?

381
00:28:21,721 --> 00:28:22,281
[SPEAKER_00]: That's insane.

382
00:28:22,341 --> 00:28:24,963
[SPEAKER_00]: That's fantasy.

383
00:28:24,983 --> 00:28:25,264
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah.

384
00:28:26,785 --> 00:28:32,710
[SPEAKER_00]: But even if, say, you could see these moment to moment, you cannot see them in that external sense.

385
00:28:33,483 --> 00:28:34,143
[SPEAKER_00]: You are them.

386
00:28:34,884 --> 00:28:43,027
[SPEAKER_00]: Your observation of it, your experience as a whole, your intention to look at these moments, remains there enduring for all this time.

387
00:28:44,567 --> 00:28:56,012
[SPEAKER_00]: Your intention to manage your suffering and refuse to allow dukkha to endure, remains there enduring as a basis for all of your other little views that flicker left and right, as you read this or you read that.

388
00:28:57,653 --> 00:28:58,233
[SPEAKER_00]: So, that's what I mean.

389
00:28:58,253 --> 00:29:05,136
[SPEAKER_00]: The honesty has to bring you back to that recognition of liability to suffer and where your intentions are.

390
00:29:05,236 --> 00:29:07,096
[SPEAKER_00]: And your intentions are not constantly changing.

391
00:29:07,876 --> 00:29:12,458
[SPEAKER_00]: If they were, you would not be able to intend anything, let alone act towards anything.

392
00:29:13,478 --> 00:29:24,422
[SPEAKER_00]: They're the glue, the binding factor that unifies your particular actions that, sure, they're changing, they're fast, because they have a greater context that remains unchanged, which is why you're still doing this,

393
00:29:24,791 --> 00:29:28,093
[SPEAKER_00]: Which is why I'm still sitting here, talking, answering questions and so on.

394
00:29:30,654 --> 00:29:49,104
[SPEAKER_01]: So, what is the way that one sees things, experiences things, you know, just without going into beliefs and just, you know, from the start you're just here, you're just here.

395
00:29:50,127 --> 00:29:55,770
[SPEAKER_00]: Well, yeah, I don't... yeah, I mean, like, you... you're gonna have some input.

396
00:29:57,471 --> 00:30:03,815
[SPEAKER_00]: So, if you just... if you dismiss any information in regard to how you're experiencing things, then you're just gonna mystify it.

397
00:30:03,835 --> 00:30:07,457
[SPEAKER_00]: It's gonna be, I'm just here, and the now-ness, and the is-ness, and the what-if-ness.

398
00:30:08,578 --> 00:30:13,921
[SPEAKER_00]: So, either way, you are not neutral when it comes to experience.

399
00:30:13,941 --> 00:30:15,722
[SPEAKER_00]: So, the question is finding the right information.

400
00:30:16,882 --> 00:30:17,763
[SPEAKER_00]: And that's the point here.

401
00:30:19,161 --> 00:30:23,004
[SPEAKER_00]: All of this is the information that shows you the contradiction of the other information.

402
00:30:24,986 --> 00:30:31,931
[SPEAKER_00]: The management, how it doesn't add up, how you cannot possibly see yourself in the external sense from which you can measure your own consciousness duration, and all of that.

403
00:30:32,651 --> 00:30:35,133
[SPEAKER_00]: Because of that, you abandon that information.

404
00:30:36,434 --> 00:30:38,516
[SPEAKER_00]: And then you find... but you need information.

405
00:30:39,217 --> 00:30:43,500
[SPEAKER_00]: One of the factors for the right view is the utterance of another, rightly understood.

406
00:30:44,691 --> 00:30:48,052
[SPEAKER_00]: But yes, you have people who say, oh, this information is just causing confusion.

407
00:30:48,092 --> 00:30:48,813
[SPEAKER_00]: It's just too much.

408
00:30:49,313 --> 00:30:50,833
[SPEAKER_00]: So, they dismiss all information.

409
00:30:51,174 --> 00:30:52,334
[SPEAKER_00]: They don't want to read the suttas.

410
00:30:52,674 --> 00:30:53,134
[SPEAKER_00]: Yes, sure.

411
00:30:55,095 --> 00:31:02,178
[SPEAKER_00]: Part of it resulted in good, because they dismiss all the wrong information, all the commentaries, all the vipassanas, all the modern stuff that's being taught.

412
00:31:02,958 --> 00:31:04,759
[SPEAKER_00]: But then, they also dismiss the right information.

413
00:31:05,179 --> 00:31:09,001
[SPEAKER_00]: So, what they're left with, then, is the complete mystification of whatever's here.

414
00:31:09,541 --> 00:31:11,842
[SPEAKER_00]: Because you don't have the means to understand it by yourself.

415
00:31:12,682 --> 00:31:14,383
[SPEAKER_00]: If you did, well, you would be samasambuddha.

416
00:31:16,652 --> 00:31:21,816
[SPEAKER_01]: But you have... so you have to develop... you have to develop some view.

417
00:31:22,457 --> 00:31:24,038
[SPEAKER_01]: You have to develop the right view.

418
00:31:24,058 --> 00:31:25,399
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah, you have to develop the right view.

419
00:31:25,440 --> 00:31:26,841
[SPEAKER_00]: You already have views.

420
00:31:27,001 --> 00:31:29,803
[SPEAKER_00]: You're not a view without any views or standpoints.

421
00:31:30,564 --> 00:31:38,811
[SPEAKER_00]: But you need to develop the right view, the right standpoint, that then, correctly, without perversion, represents your experience.

422
00:31:40,973 --> 00:31:43,435
[SPEAKER_00]: But your experience is always going to be known through views.

423
00:31:44,229 --> 00:32:02,764
[SPEAKER_00]: So, that's why then you realize, with that honest transparency, that if I truly want to be free from suffering then, I need to find the teaching that does not provide another explanation for what's happening to me and another belief and another kind of, oh, this is how it is and that's it, adopt this and you're fine because everything fits.

424
00:32:03,245 --> 00:32:06,548
[SPEAKER_00]: No, I need to find instructions that will make me basically

425
00:32:07,913 --> 00:32:10,435
[SPEAKER_00]: Not be able to experience suffering in the first place.

426
00:32:11,015 --> 00:32:17,158
[SPEAKER_00]: And then, if you take that view already... See, that's already like a right view for a patujana, because he already knows where to look.

427
00:32:18,379 --> 00:32:19,800
[SPEAKER_00]: So then, that person would then...

428
00:32:20,160 --> 00:32:29,345
[SPEAKER_00]: He would not be too far-fetched to then see how virtue, taming of the senses, solitude... How is that fortifying your mind?

429
00:32:30,125 --> 00:32:34,148
[SPEAKER_00]: You realize, oh, so it's not about just another explanation, just another belief.

430
00:32:34,992 --> 00:32:44,977
[SPEAKER_00]: It's about training and undoing this being here, that when touched by feeling, it suffers, it resists, it wants more, and all of that.

431
00:32:45,417 --> 00:32:52,280
[SPEAKER_00]: So, it's dismantling those reactions, those habits, those, basically, assumptions in regard to that.

432
00:32:53,320 --> 00:32:54,641
[SPEAKER_00]: And you say, oh, yeah, how would you start?

433
00:32:54,681 --> 00:32:58,583
[SPEAKER_00]: Well, by taming, by constricting the wrong behavior.

434
00:32:59,390 --> 00:33:02,592
[SPEAKER_00]: By taming, by moderating eating, by speech, by all of that.

435
00:33:03,152 --> 00:33:04,954
[SPEAKER_00]: And then, the mind becomes immovable.

436
00:33:06,214 --> 00:33:07,675
[SPEAKER_00]: And then, you get to see these things.

437
00:33:07,835 --> 00:33:08,576
[SPEAKER_00]: And then, you realize, oh, yeah.

438
00:33:08,916 --> 00:33:14,019
[SPEAKER_00]: When you understood then, that whatever touches you, whatever is manifested, that's why it's impermanent.

439
00:33:14,199 --> 00:33:15,480
[SPEAKER_00]: Because it came on its own.

440
00:33:16,401 --> 00:33:19,102
[SPEAKER_00]: Not because you saw it ending, or because this, or you explained it.

441
00:33:19,122 --> 00:33:20,723
[SPEAKER_00]: And then, you realize, I don't need to do anything.

442
00:33:21,424 --> 00:33:22,745
[SPEAKER_00]: For as long as I'm alive,

443
00:33:23,669 --> 00:33:29,335
[SPEAKER_00]: Anything that I experience, I will be reminded of my freedom from it, through that experience.

444
00:33:30,276 --> 00:33:43,310
[SPEAKER_01]: So, this is a sutta from the Dīgha Nikaya, and this is where King Mahāsūdāsanā is passing away, and he's... the Queen visits him on his deathbed.

445
00:33:44,460 --> 00:33:51,401
[SPEAKER_01]: And she says, Sāyā, you have 84,000 cities with the royal capital of Kusavati foremost.

446
00:33:51,941 --> 00:33:53,181
[SPEAKER_01]: Arouse desire for these.

447
00:33:53,301 --> 00:33:54,562
[SPEAKER_01]: Take an interest in life.

448
00:33:55,502 --> 00:33:56,682
[SPEAKER_01]: All these wonderful things that you have.

449
00:33:56,702 --> 00:33:57,262
[SPEAKER_01]: And he's got all...

450
00:33:57,342 --> 00:33:58,082
[SPEAKER_01]: Possessions.

451
00:33:58,162 --> 00:33:58,922
[SPEAKER_01]: All these possessions.

452
00:33:58,942 --> 00:33:59,463
[SPEAKER_01]: Got everything.

453
00:33:59,803 --> 00:34:02,163
[SPEAKER_01]: And he rules with kindness.

454
00:34:03,743 --> 00:34:09,584
[SPEAKER_01]: And she likewise urged the king to live on by taking an interest in all his possessions as described above.

455
00:34:09,824 --> 00:34:12,145
[SPEAKER_00]: Army, and horses, and palaces, and wives.

456
00:34:12,165 --> 00:34:12,965
[SPEAKER_00]: Jewels and everything.

457
00:34:13,025 --> 00:34:13,285
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah.

458
00:34:13,485 --> 00:34:13,845
[SPEAKER_00]: Wealth.

459
00:34:13,905 --> 00:34:14,145
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah.

460
00:34:15,064 --> 00:34:23,072
[SPEAKER_01]: And when the queen had spoken, the king said to her, for a long time, my queen, you have spoken to me with loving, desirable, pleasant and agreeable words.

461
00:34:23,112 --> 00:34:28,518
[SPEAKER_01]: And yet, in my final hour, your words are undesirable, unpleasant and disagreeable.

462
00:34:30,180 --> 00:34:33,903
[SPEAKER_01]: Then she said to him, then how exactly, your majesty, am I to speak to you?

463
00:34:35,085 --> 00:34:36,786
[SPEAKER_01]: And he says, like this, my queen,

464
00:34:37,784 --> 00:34:42,246
[SPEAKER_01]: Sāyya, we must be parted and separated from all we hold dear and beloved.

465
00:34:42,286 --> 00:34:43,826
[SPEAKER_01]: Don't pass away with concerns.

466
00:34:44,667 --> 00:34:47,648
[SPEAKER_01]: Such concern is suffering and it is criticized.

467
00:34:48,388 --> 00:34:53,630
[SPEAKER_01]: Sāyya, you have 84,000 cities with the royal capital of Kusuvati foremost.

468
00:34:53,650 --> 00:34:54,830
[SPEAKER_01]: Give up desire for these.

469
00:34:54,990 --> 00:34:56,811
[SPEAKER_01]: Take no interest in life."

470
00:34:56,831 --> 00:34:56,991
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah.

471
00:34:57,251 --> 00:34:58,952
[SPEAKER_01]: And so on for all of the king's possessions.

472
00:34:58,972 --> 00:35:02,013
[SPEAKER_00]: Before the life ends, we choose to holding on to them.

473
00:35:02,273 --> 00:35:03,994
[SPEAKER_00]: You see, there's a suffering there.

474
00:35:04,654 --> 00:35:06,175
[SPEAKER_01]: So, when the king had spoken, the queen,

475
00:35:10,436 --> 00:35:29,112
[SPEAKER_01]: At the end of that sutta, the Buddha goes on to say that famous verse, all determinations, all conditions are impermanent.

476
00:35:29,353 --> 00:35:30,634
[SPEAKER_01]: All determinations are impermanent.

477
00:35:30,954 --> 00:35:32,075
[SPEAKER_01]: Their nature is to

478
00:35:33,111 --> 00:35:34,532
[SPEAKER_01]: Arise and cease.

479
00:35:35,553 --> 00:35:38,275
[SPEAKER_01]: Having arisen, they will have to cease.

480
00:35:38,355 --> 00:35:39,296
[SPEAKER_01]: They will have to come to an end.

481
00:35:39,316 --> 00:35:41,038
[SPEAKER_01]: Their stilling is true bliss.

482
00:35:42,279 --> 00:35:45,221
[SPEAKER_00]: Their stilling, not their cessation.

483
00:35:47,463 --> 00:35:48,204
[SPEAKER_00]: Their stilling.

484
00:35:48,224 --> 00:35:48,464
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah.

485
00:35:48,944 --> 00:35:50,766
[SPEAKER_00]: Stilling of them while they're there.

486
00:35:50,966 --> 00:35:51,607
[SPEAKER_00]: That's the point.

487
00:35:51,847 --> 00:35:56,270
[SPEAKER_00]: So, before things have ceased for that king, i.e.

488
00:35:56,350 --> 00:35:59,233
[SPEAKER_00]: before he died, before things were ripped away,

489
00:36:01,668 --> 00:36:17,362
[SPEAKER_00]: He's advising himself, through the Queen, to abandon desire towards those determinations, towards those possessions, to still them, to let them be, so to speak, to remove his emotional involvement, ownership, satisfaction, safety, and everything else.

490
00:36:18,523 --> 00:36:20,105
[SPEAKER_01]: Because if that's not done, there is no...

491
00:36:20,305 --> 00:36:28,312
[SPEAKER_00]: So, then, the point is, like, okay, so, do you practicing stilling of your ownership, or do you just, sort of, trying to, to, kind of,

492
00:36:28,920 --> 00:36:33,041
[SPEAKER_00]: Inject the view that nothing is permanent, because it would... it would disappear.

493
00:36:33,441 --> 00:36:34,121
[SPEAKER_00]: That's not stealing.

494
00:36:34,141 --> 00:36:35,402
[SPEAKER_00]: That's just another cessation.

495
00:36:36,802 --> 00:36:39,002
[SPEAKER_00]: So, you want to steal things before they cease.

496
00:36:41,023 --> 00:36:46,084
[SPEAKER_00]: Stealing of saṅkharas is the peace, is the bliss, is nibbāna.

497
00:36:46,804 --> 00:36:50,225
[SPEAKER_01]: Not cessation of them, because that's still saṃsāra.

498
00:36:52,226 --> 00:36:54,066
[SPEAKER_00]: They cease here, they rise somewhere else.

499
00:36:58,590 --> 00:37:13,935
[SPEAKER_00]: So, again, even that whole sutta rests, the whole notion of anicca, the whole notion of impermanence, on the level of possessions, on the level of things that you desire, that provide satisfaction, that provide zeal, inspiration, will to live.

500
00:37:15,275 --> 00:37:16,835
[SPEAKER_00]: So, you're still in that desire.

501
00:37:17,456 --> 00:37:25,478
[SPEAKER_00]: You're not denying the reality and the existence of those things through your secondary hindsight contrived explanations.

